{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792959101,"nonce":"Jx/unBKQYh5M2CeA/7qAO5bzqDA=","message":"Microsandbox feasibility and revised execution boundary (2026-10-25)\n\nAssessment: viable as an external compute provider for a workspace session. A Durable Object coordinates that provider; the Microsandbox runtime does not execute inside the Durable Object's Workers isolate. Linux self-hosting needs KVM. Cloudflare's own Containers already place each instance in a Firecracker microVM, so nested Microsandbox is not a prerequisite and nested virtualization must not be assumed.\n\nRevise the original Cloudflare-Containers-only execution proposal to a provider seam. Keep the existing session protocol and FileStore/Programs contracts. Candidate providers are Cloudflare Containers and a trusted Microsandbox host/broker on KVM-capable infrastructure. Managed Microsandbox Cloud is a conditional option: access is private beta; lifecycle management has an HTTPS API, while command/file/PTY traffic uses its command channel. Validate that channel from Workers or use a broker; do not assume the native SDK runs in a Durable Object. Cloud does not accept custom published ports, and its snapshots and metrics differ from the local runtime, so preview ingress, restart and metering need explicit proof.\n\nOwnership:\n- One authenticated workspace identity maps to its Durable Object, with session membership/roles, CRDT update log/checkpoints, compute lease and quota accounting. Authorized collaborators in the same workspace share the environment.\n- Each active workspace gets its own isolated execution environment for Rayzor/LSP, Haxe/Ash/Caribou, PTYs and extension processes. Independent workspaces do not share writable mounts, process namespaces or credentials.\n- Treat VM disk as a materialized workspace and build cache. Document edits remain under CRDT authority, and use explicit flush/checkpoint/version barriers before builds, snapshots and suspension. Build output and file-watcher reconciliation must not create a second document authority.\n- IndexedDB is an offline/recovery cache. Scope cached documents to immutable workspace identity plus server/account context and document identity, not workspace display name or relative path alone. Browser picker and file moves operate on the remote session; backend Linux must not select frontend platform behavior.\n\nValidation gates before choosing MSB for production:\n1. Provision and destroy two environments through the same runner API; verify cross-workspace files, credentials, process control and network access are refused.\n2. Stream PTYs with resize/backpressure and disconnection; run actual Haxe/Ash/Rayzor and approved extension workloads.\n3. Reconnect two CRDT clients, checkpoint, kill/recreate compute and restore without lost acknowledged edits or revived deleted/renamed paths. Rename of open documents needs stable document identity; the current path-based guard is temporary.\n4. Prove authenticated preview ingress and bounded egress without exposing host metadata or another tenant's services.\n5. Measure cold start, idle suspension/resume, CPU/RAM/disk accounting, concurrency caps, artifact retention and cleanup after failure. Ensure compute wake/lease ownership prevents duplicate active writers.\n6. Verify SDK/broker transport compatibility, provider availability, current pricing and resource limits. Benchmark self-hosted MSB against Cloudflare Containers before committing to a default.\n\nSources checked:\nhttps://github.com/superradcompany/microsandbox#readme\nhttps://docs.microsandbox.dev/cloud/overview\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/containers/concepts/architecture/\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/sandbox/\n\nRelated coordination/auth runner: git-bug 52138fcda83502ec87679713955f7cf7c7f7e8654a0ef55ba60357bd0f22a904. Keep this issue open: this is a feasible design direction with validation gates, not a shipped Microsandbox integration.","files":null}]}